# Build-context exclusions for the production image (Dockerfile,
# docs/deployment.md §2). Two jobs: keep the context small, and make it
# impossible for host-side build artifacts or secrets to enter an image.

# Dependencies and build output are produced inside the image, never copied
# from the host — a stale local dist/ leaking in would ship code that does not
# match the committed sources.
node_modules/
**/node_modules/
dist/
**/dist/
coverage/

# Both forms are required. Unlike .gitignore, a leading-wildcard
# .dockerignore pattern matches only at the context root — "*.tsbuildinfo"
# alone lets packages/*/tsconfig.tsbuildinfo through, and TypeScript then
# trusts that stale incremental manifest, reports every project up to date,
# and emits NOTHING. The build "succeeds" with an empty dist and the failure
# only surfaces later, as an unresolvable @backburner/engine import.
*.tsbuildinfo
**/*.tsbuildinfo

# Secrets. .env is gitignored, but the Docker context is not git — this is the
# line that actually keeps it out of the image (docs/deployment.md §6). The
# **/ forms cover a per-package .env (Vite reads one) for the same reason the
# tsbuildinfo entry above needs them.
.env
.env.*
**/.env
**/.env.*
!.env.example

# Not needed to build or run: history, CI config, the other environments'
# definitions, and the design corpus (which carries the assessment PDF).
.git/
.github/
.devcontainer/
docs/
*.md
!README.md

# Tests never run in the image; the e2e package is present only so npm ci can
# satisfy the lockfile's workspace set.
**/test/
packages/e2e/.logs/

# Editor and OS noise.
.obsidian/
.vscode/
.idea/
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
